ed earnings of  nearly a quarter trillion dollars  that's literally 34 billion dollars more  than apple and google combined  also we shouldn't note that saudi  aramco's investors don't get to keep  most of this money like apple and google  saudi aramco has to hand over roughly  half of their profits straight to the  saudi government with such numbers  though it's no wonder why the saudi  government is so rich but while these  numbers are super glamorous the same  cannot be said about the lives of saudi  immigrants the vast majority of saudi  immigrants are from third world  countries that are there to do hard  labor this includes countries like egypt  pakistan india bangladesh yemen and  syria now this might be a bit confusing  why take all this risk and emigrate to  arguably one of the scariest countries  in the world just to be paid a minimum  wage well the reality is that these  immigrants lives in their home countries  are simply far worse  most of them are usually jobless and  can't even carry on daily life so saudi  arabia's relatively low labor salaries  actually seem pretty appealing to many  of them technically saudi arabia does  have a minimum wage of four thousand  realms per month or roughly a thousand  dollars but this only applies to saudi  citizens and immigrants are usually paid  less the average labor in saudi arabia  is paid 36 thousand reals per year or  just under 10 000  something to note however is that  companies usually take care of housing  food and other common living expenses so  it's actually quite possible to save  over 90 of your salary on an annual  basis also most immigrants don't stay  here for several years or decades they  usually come on three to five year  contracts save up 25 to 35 000 and head  back home where they now have a small  fortune take india for example where 30  000 american dollars is equivalent to  roughly 2.4 million rupees this amount  of money is basically impossible to  achieve as a hard laborer in india in  fact this is not a small sum of money  even for white-collar workers in india  like software engineers the average  software engineer in india i earn 600  000 rupees per year so it would take  four years just to earn 2.4 million  rupees if we assume that these engineers  could save up 20 of their income it  would take them 20 years to build up 2.4  million dollars now of course it's not  like these guys will be stuck at 600 000  rupees per year for 20 years but i think  you get the point for poor individuals  from such countries taking on a labor  job in saudi arabia is actually super  lucrative but this money definitely does  not come easy as laborers in saudi  arabia are often subject to loads of  atrocities  starting off with the lighter end of  atrocities we have overworking as a hard  laborer it's extremely unlikely that  you're only going to be working at eight  hours per day  more times than not you'll be working 10  to 12 hours for at least six days a week  for certain industries like the  restaurant industry these numbers even  go up to 16 hours per day now saudi  arabia does technically have rules that  are supposed to prevent this from  happening for example working hours  aren't supposed to exceed 60 hours per  week and there are limits to overtime as  well  but if we're being honest it's not like  hard laborers are gonna go and report  their employers for them they can't risk  losing their jobs or worse their freedom  you see it employers often confiscate  the passports of laborers as soon as  they arrive and the passports aren't  returned until the term is over this  ensures that these workers won't try to  run away or report their employers  before their term is over  this too is illegal according to saudi  law but that doesn't mean that it's  uncommon something else that's pretty  common is discrimination race and  gender-based discrimination is rather  common but just being a labor itself is  usually enough to receive poor treatment  while none of this is optimal if you  keep your head down and just do what  you're asked to you should be able to  complete your term without any issues  but for some laborers things take a turn  for the worst the criminal justice  system in saudi arabia isn't exactly  fair so if an employer reports labor for  some sort of crime the laborer is  basically screwed let's just say saudi  arabia has some questionable ways of  getting confessions and they usually  have severe punishments for even the  smallest offenses the ones that are  lucky are able to get away with a couple  of years in jail and a few hundred  lashes but the ones that aren't so lucky  well they aren't so lucky so it's best  that you stay on the good side of your  employer regardless of how big of a  douchebag they really are given that  saudi arabia is trying to open up the  western tourists and basically become  another dubai they have put more effort  into fixing these injustices over the  past few years for example they've added  stipulations that allow laborers to  switch jobs without their employer's  consent similarly laborers no longer  need approval from their employers to  leave or re-enter the country while  these are definitely good steps in the  right direction and if laborers don't  take advantage of these opportunities it  doesn't really mean anything also just  because it's getting better in certain  areas it doesn't mean that's getting  better in all areas for example within  the first nine months of 2021 41 kenyans  died in mysterious circumstances and  there were over a thousand laborers in  distress hearing all this you probably  think that only the most desperate end  up making their way to saudi arabia and  that's true for basically all laborers  in the country but there is a small  portion of immigrants that come there  purely out of free will and here's why  while conditions aren't that great for  hard laborers the same cannot be said  about educated immigrants like engineers  doctors nurses teachers scientists and  so on these skills are highly valued  virtually everywhere and it's no  different than saudi arabia  in fact 85 of engineers in saudi arabia  are expats so clearly saudi arabia is  quite appealing for white-collar workers  as well  now it's not like engineers from western  countries are fighting tooth and nail to  try to get into saudi arabia but the  same cannot be said about engineers from  developing countries saudi arabia  doesn't pay insane salaries like feng or  something but they do have white-collar  salaries that are basically on par with  most western countries take petroleum  engineers for example the average  petroleum engineer at saudi aramco earns  109 000 per year meanwhile the average  petroleum engineer in india earned 811  000 rupees or just about ten thousand  dollars even a top petroleum engineer in  india only earns four million rupees or  about fifty thousand dollars so when  these engineers are offered a salary  that's two to ten times more than their  salaries in india it's no wonder why  they take the deal also something else  that we should note is that there is no  personal income tax in saudi arabia for  saudi arabia it simply makes more sense  to tax a couple of gas companies another  two percent then try attacks millions of  residents 20 or 30 percent with that  being said 110 000 salary in saudi  arabia is actually equivalent to about  150 000 in america it's not just  engineers and doctors that are attracted  to saudi arabia either  they're also able to attract a bunch of  managers directors executives and  business leaders for example 18 of ceos  at saudi companies are actually  foreigners in terms of living conditions  for these white-collar workers they're  obviously way better than hard laborers  they usually don't have to worry about  employers confiscating their passports  or having to work 80 plus hours every  single week  according to most accounts it seems that  life for these workers is very similar  to how life would have been if they were  in a western country with the same job  there are of course a few local  differences for example you have regular  prayer breaks and you're not going to be  drinking wine or eating pork but for the  most part things aren't all that  different you shouldn't mistake the  similarities for complete freedom though  if you break a law you're still gonna  have a far worse experience than in any  western country but that's just part of  the deal if there weren't any added  risks saudi arabia obviously wouldn't  have to pay as much so professional  expats don't tend to stay here for  decades either but they do tend to stay  for longer than laborers  in the end a large number of immigrants  in saudi arabia just comes down to one  thing and that's jobs having a massive  oil reserve it's only natural that the  country has plenty of opportunities  across the board  for a lot of westerners these  opportunities often don't seem to be  worth it given the associated risk of  living in the country and the ethical  concerns of supporting the country  but for a lot of individuals from  developing countries they don't have the  luxury to make such a choice going to  saudi arabia and grinding out a few  years of hard labor is often times the  most straightforward way to escape  poverty now of course the same cannot be  said about the white-collar workers  while they may be able to earn a lot  more in saudi arabia it's not like they  would have starved in their home  countries either so they did have the  luxury of making the choice and they  chose saudi arabia i know this will  probably come up as greedy unethical and  immoral to many of you but everybody's  experiences and perspectives are  different and you can't really  understand the situation or reasoning  without having lived their lives but  that's just what i think would you be  willing to work in saudi arabia for two  hundred thousand dollars per year  comment that down below also drop a l